{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788351358,"nonce":"Mfzt6o4iHV4wSlcIMzvrJEFuAx0=","message":"**Resolved before dispatch, orchestrator, repair round 2026-09-02.** This issue needs per-element state age, and where that age LIVES is a real decision the acceptance text does not make. Settled so no worker has to guess mid-implementation.\n\n**The constraint.** `AgentGameComponent` is a `GameComponent` with **no `ExposeData`** — grep it. Nothing it holds survives a save/load. Run `m1-20260901` spanned sessions and bench relaunches, and `664e9b9` explicitly allows that, so any in-memory stall history is going to be incomplete in exactly the runs this issue exists to serve.\n\n**Do NOT add scribed state to solve it.** Persisting a tracking dictionary via `Scribe_Collections` is the obvious move and it is the wrong one here: writing scribed state from the observation surface is a live hazard class in this project (`d16a463` is an open issue about observers writing scribed state, and `_mp/DETERMINISM.md` documents the family). Buying stall-history durability with new scribed state is not a trade this round should make.\n\n**The resolution: track in memory, and publish what you do not know.**\n\n- `state_since_tick` / `state_age_ticks` where the mod has actually observed the transition.\n- Alongside them, a `tracked_since` tick saying when this process started watching. On a fresh load it is `now`, and it climbs from there.\n- A reader can then distinguish three states that must never collapse into each other: **stalled** (observed in this state across N day boundaries), **not stalled** (observed changing), and **not known yet** (tracking has not covered N day boundaries).\n\nThat third state is the whole point. Per [[acceptance-suites-must-prove-shapes]], an absent or zero age must not read as \"clean\" — that is precisely how the original defect worked, where a flat number read as a healthy one. A reload resetting the clock is acceptable; a reload silently reporting \"not stalled\" is not.\n\n**Consequence for acceptance item 2.** The `stalled[]` roll-up is a floor, not a census, for the first N day boundaries after any load, and the envelope should say so in the same idiom `construction`'s existing `cap_note` already uses — presence is the signal, so the note appears only when tracking is too young to answer.\n\nThis also interacts with `bb931b9` (the save verb, in flight this round): a run that checkpoints and resumes will reset stall tracking, which is fine and expected under the above, and is worth one line in whatever documentation this lands with.","files":null}]}